<!--
  A separate POLICY (not another AssignVariable block bolted onto
  AM-Extract-Client-Cert-TLS), and that split is the actual fix, not an
  optional cleanup: on a real trace, a later AssignVariable block reading a
  variable that an EARLIER block in that SAME AssignMessage had just set
  (client.cert.base64.step1) came back unresolved, while a block reading a
  real pre-existing flow variable (tls.client.raw.cert) worked fine in that
  same policy. A variable set by one policy IS reliably visible to the next
  policy STEP in the flow, so each chained transformation gets its own
  policy instead of trusting cross-block visibility within one AssignMessage;
  that visibility gap is exactly the class of undocumented behavior this
  whole bundle has repeatedly had to learn the hard way, empirically.

  Removes the collapsed BEGINCERTIFICATE marker as ONE literal, no space needed:
  client.cert.base64.step1 (from AM-Extract-Client-Cert-TLS) already had
  every whitespace character stripped, including the space that used to sit
  between "BEGIN" and "CERTIFICATE" in the original PEM text.
-->
<AssignMessage name="AM-Extract-Client-Cert-Strip-Step2">
    <DisplayName>AM Extract Client Cert Strip Step2</DisplayName>
    <AssignVariable>
        <Name>client.cert.base64.step2</Name>
        <Template>{replaceAll(client.cert.base64.step1,'-----BEGINCERTIFICATE-----','')}</Template>
    </AssignVariable>
    <IgnoreUnresolvedVariables>true</IgnoreUnresolvedVariables>
</AssignMessage>
